            ("Indri", "Indri songs follow rhythmic patterns similar to human music, with predictable timing intervals. They are one of the few animals with a sense of rhythm.", "indri.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Mouse Lemur
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs are among the smallest primates in the world, weighing just 30-70 grams - about the same as a couple of AA batteries.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs can enter a state of torpor during the dry season, lowering their metabolism to survive when food is scarce, reducing energy use by up to 90%.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs have enormous eyes relative to their head size, giving them exceptional night vision for hunting insects in the dark.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "There are at least 25 recognized species of mouse lemur, with new species still being discovered. Many look identical but are genetically distinct.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs are one of the most important models for studying primate evolution because they closely resemble the earliest primates that lived 60 million years ago.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            # Sifaka (Verreaux's)
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Sifakas cannot walk on all fours. Instead, they hop sideways on their hind legs when crossing open ground, looking like tiny ballet dancers.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Sifakas can leap up to 10 meters between trees, using their powerful hind legs like springs. They rotate in mid-air to land feet-first.", "sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "The name 'sifaka' comes from their alarm call - a loud 'shee-FAK!' that echoes through the forest when they spot a predator.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Baby sifakas begin practicing their famous sideways dance hop within weeks of birth, clinging to their mothers as they leap between trees.", "sifaka.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # Red Ruffed Lemur
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs are one of the few primates that build nests for their young, and they can have litters of up to six babies - the largest of any lemur.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs are critical pollinators of the traveller's palm tree. They push their snouts deep into flowers to drink nectar, transferring pollen.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs have a specialized 'toilet claw' on their second toe, used for grooming their thick, luxurious fur.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Found only on the Masoala Peninsula, their entire wild habitat is smaller than the city of Los Angeles.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "conservation"),
            # Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Black-and-white ruffed lemurs are the largest of the true lemur family and have the loudest call of any primate relative to their body size.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "They are the primary pollinator of the traveller's tree (Ravenala madagascariensis), Madagascar's national plant.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Unlike most primates, black-and-white ruffed lemurs park their babies in nests instead of carrying them, returning periodically to nurse.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Their black-and-white pattern varies between individuals like a fingerprint. No two ruffed lemurs have exactly the same markings.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # Blue-eyed Black Lemur
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "The blue-eyed black lemur is one of the only primates other than humans to have truly blue eyes - a remarkably rare trait in the animal kingdom.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Male blue-eyed black lemurs are entirely black, while females are orange-brown. They're so different-looking that they were once classified as separate species.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Fewer than 1,000 blue-eyed black lemurs remain in the wild, confined to a tiny area of northwestern Madagascar smaller than a mid-sized city.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Blue-eyed black lemurs are important seed dispersers. Some seeds only germinate after passing through their digestive system.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "ecology"),
            # Coquerel's Sifaka
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Coquerel's sifakas have a specialized digestive system with an enlarged cecum that allows them to eat toxic plants that would be lethal to other animals.", "sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Coquerel's sifakas are featured on the PBS children's show 'Zoboomafoo', making them one of the most famous lemur species in American pop culture.", "sifaka.jpg", "culture"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "These sifakas scent-mark trees by rubbing their chest glands against bark, creating an invisible map of territory boundaries.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "The fat-tailed dwarf lemur is the only primate known to truly hibernate, storing fat in its tail to survive for up to seven months.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "During hibernation, a fat-tailed dwarf lemur's body temperature fluctuates with ambient temperature, something no other primate does.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "Their tail can account for up to 40% of their body weight before hibernation - it's essentially a built-in energy backpack.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "Scientists study fat-tailed dwarf lemurs to understand human hibernation potential, which could be revolutionary for long-duration space travel.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            # Crowned Lemur
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs get their name from the striking orange-brown triangular patch on the top of their head, resembling a tiny crown.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs can coexist peacefully with Sanford's brown lemurs in the same forests, something rare among closely related primate species.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Female crowned lemurs are grey with an orange crown, while males are darker brown with a black and orange crown - a striking example of sexual dimorphism.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs have been observed using millipedes as a natural insect repellent, rubbing the toxic arthropods on their fur to ward off parasites.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Golden Bamboo Lemur
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "The golden bamboo lemur eats enough cyanide daily in bamboo shoots to kill most animals. A single day's intake could kill a human, yet it suffers no ill effects.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "The golden bamboo lemur wasn't discovered until 1987, proving that even large primates can remain hidden in Madagascar's dense rainforests.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "Fewer than 1,000 golden bamboo lemurs exist in the wild, almost entirely within Ranomafana National Park, which was established partly to protect them.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "Scientists still don't understand how golden bamboo lemurs detoxify cyanide. Understanding this could lead to breakthroughs in treating cyanide poisoning in humans.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            # Sportive Lemur
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Despite their name, sportive lemurs are among the least active primates, sleeping up to 18 hours a day to conserve energy on their low-calorie leaf diet.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Sportive lemurs practice cecotrophy - they eat their own feces to extract additional nutrients, similar to rabbits. This lets them survive on leaves alone.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "There are 26 recognized species of sportive lemur, the most species-rich genus of lemurs. Many were only distinguished through genetic analysis.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Sportive lemurs sleep in tree holes during the day and are so sedentary that researchers can find the same individual in the same hole for months.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Eastern Woolly Lemur
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Eastern woolly lemurs form lifelong monogamous pair bonds, one of the few lemur species to do so. Partners sleep huddled together every day.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Their dense, woolly fur acts like a natural insulation jacket, allowing them to thrive in cool mountain forests where temperatures can drop near freezing.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Woolly lemurs communicate through a variety of calls, including a distinctive 'avahy' sound that gives them their scientific genus name, Avahi.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Fork-marked Lemur
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Fork-marked lemurs are specialized gum-feeders with reinforced tooth combs that can gouge bark, allowing access to tree sap unavailable to other lemurs.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "The distinctive dark stripe on a fork-marked lemur's face splits into a Y-shape on the forehead, giving the species its common name.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Fork-marked lemurs have an unusually large brain for their body size compared to other lemurs, possibly related to their complex foraging behavior.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # Diademed Sifaka
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "The diademed sifaka is often called the most beautiful of all lemurs, with silky fur in stunning patches of gold, white, grey, and black.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Diademed sifakas are one of the rarest lemurs, with fewer than 6,000-10,000 remaining. Habitat loss to slash-and-burn farming is their greatest threat.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Diademed sifakas practice 'sunning' behavior - sitting on branches with arms spread wide to absorb warmth, looking like they're meditating.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Unlike many lemurs, diademed sifakas rarely come to the ground. They spend almost their entire lives in the rainforest canopy, 20-30 meters above the forest floor.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Black Lemur
            ("Black Lemur", "Black lemurs are one of the few lemur species that have adapted to living near humans, sometimes raiding crops and surviving in fragmented forest patches.", "black_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Black Lemur", "On the island of Nosy Komba, black lemurs have become so habituated to tourists that they will climb onto visitors' shoulders to eat bananas.", "black_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Black Lemur", "Male black lemurs are entirely jet black, while females are chestnut brown. Early scientists thought they were two different species.", "black_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # Mongoose Lemur
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "The mongoose lemur is one of only two lemur species found outside Madagascar - small populations live on the Comoros Islands, likely brought by ancient seafarers.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Mongoose lemurs switch between being active during the day (wet season) and active at night (dry season), one of the few primates to shift their activity pattern.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Mongoose lemurs have a mutualistic relationship with certain flowers - they are important pollinators of kapok and baobab trees while feeding on nectar.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            # Pygmy Mouse Lemur
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "The pygmy mouse lemur is the smallest primate in the world by average body mass, weighing around 30 grams - less than a slice of bread.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Pygmy mouse lemurs were described in 1852 but then went missing for over 100 years, only being rediscovered in 1993 in western Madagascar.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Despite their minuscule size, pygmy mouse lemurs are territorial and males will aggressively defend their feeding trees from rivals.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "A pygmy mouse lemur's heart beats up to 800 times per minute, but during torpor it can slow to just 8 beats per minute.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Ring-tailed Lemur ---
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "Ring-tailed lemurs have scent glands on their wrists and shoulders that produce a complex cocktail of over 300 chemical compounds used in communication.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "A group of ring-tailed lemurs is called a 'conspiracy'. Troops typically contain 6 to 30 individuals.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "social"),
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "Ring-tailed lemurs purr like cats when content, and they have over 28 distinct vocalizations for different situations.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "Female ring-tailed lemurs only have a 12-hour window each year in which they can conceive, making successful breeding remarkably difficult.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Aye-aye ---
            ("Aye-aye", "The aye-aye is the only primate with continuously growing incisors, a trait it shares with rodents. Its teeth grow about 2-3 mm per month.", "aye_aye.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Aye-aye", "Aye-ayes build elaborate sleeping nests from leaves and branches high in trees, and they may maintain up to 20 different nests within their territory.", "aye_aye.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Aye-aye", "A single aye-aye can tap on wood up to 8 times per second while foraging, listening for the echo that reveals hollow chambers containing insect larvae.", "aye_aye.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Aye-aye", "Aye-ayes have the largest brain-to-body ratio of any lemur, which may relate to the complex sensory processing required for their percussive foraging technique.", "aye_aye.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Indri ---
            ("Indri", "Indri duets between mated pairs can last up to 3 minutes and include precise rhythmic coordination that scientists compare to jazz improvisation.", "indri.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Indri", "Female indris only give birth every 2-3 years, making their population recovery extremely slow even when habitat is protected.", "indri.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Indri", "Indris are strict herbivores that eat over 70 different plant species, selecting young leaves that are higher in protein and lower in toxins.", "indri.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Indri", "The indri's hind legs are significantly longer than its arms, an adaptation for its vertical clinging and leaping locomotion through the forest.", "indri.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Mouse Lemur ---
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Female mouse lemurs nest communally, with up to 15 females and their young sharing a single tree hole for warmth and protection.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs have a gestation period of only 60 days and can produce twins or triplets, giving them the fastest reproduction rate of any primate.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs store fat in their hind legs and tail base before the dry season, increasing their body weight by up to 50% in preparation for torpor.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "The grey mouse lemur shares about 95% of its DNA with humans, making it a valuable model for studying Alzheimer's disease and aging.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Mouse lemurs can hear ultrasonic frequencies up to 90 kHz, far beyond human hearing range, which they use in social communication.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Verreaux's Sifaka ---
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Verreaux's sifakas spend about 40% of their day resting, 30% feeding, and the remainder traveling and socializing.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Female sifakas are dominant over males and get priority access to food. Males must wait until females have finished eating.", "sifaka.jpg", "social"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Sifakas have a specialized gut with an enlarged cecum that ferments leaves, similar to a cow's rumen, allowing them to extract nutrients from tough foliage.", "sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Verreaux's sifakas sunbathe each morning with their arms outstretched in what researchers call the 'sun worship' posture to raise their body temperature.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "Infant mortality in Verreaux's sifakas can exceed 50% in their first year, primarily due to predation by fossas and raptors.", "sifaka.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Red Ruffed Lemur ---
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs are among the most vocal primates, with an alarm call that can be heard over a kilometer away through dense forest.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Mother red ruffed lemurs leave their babies in the nest while foraging, returning every 2-3 hours to nurse - a behavior called 'infant parking'.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs can hang upside down by their feet to reach fruit on thin branches that wouldn't support their weight otherwise.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "The red ruffed lemur's population has declined by more than 80% over the past 24 years due to logging and hunting on the Masoala Peninsula.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Red ruffed lemurs have a unique 'communal crèche' system where multiple mothers may leave their infants together in a shared nest.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "social"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur ---
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Black-and-white ruffed lemurs have a gestation period of about 102 days, one of the shortest among lemurs of their size.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "These lemurs have a specialized 'wrist gland' that produces scent marks, which they rub on branches to communicate territory boundaries.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Black-and-white ruffed lemurs form the largest social groups of any lemur, with communities of up to 31 individuals that split and merge throughout the day.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "social"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Their diet of primarily ripe fruit makes them critical seed dispersers - they can disperse seeds up to 200 meters from the parent tree.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Black-and-white Ruffed Lemur", "Female black-and-white ruffed lemurs can give birth to up to 6 offspring at once, the largest litter size of any primate.", "bw_ruffed.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Blue-eyed Black Lemur ---
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Blue-eyed black lemurs live in groups of 4-11 individuals, usually with more males than females - unusual among lemurs where females typically dominate.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "social"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "These lemurs are cathemeral, meaning they are active both day and night, adjusting their activity patterns based on season, temperature, and food availability.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "The blue eye color in these lemurs is caused by a low concentration of melanin in the iris, the same genetic mechanism that produces blue eyes in humans.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Blue-eyed black lemurs use their hands to form a cup shape to drink water from tree holes, one of the few lemurs observed using this technique.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Blue-eyed Black Lemur", "Habitat destruction has fragmented their remaining forest into patches, with some populations now consisting of fewer than 30 individuals.", "blue_eyed.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Coquerel's Sifaka ---
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Coquerel's sifakas can leap distances of over 8 meters between trees, rotating their bodies 180 degrees mid-air to land facing the trunk.", "sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Female Coquerel's sifakas give birth to a single infant after a 5-month gestation. The baby clings to its mother's belly for the first month.", "sifaka.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Coquerel's sifakas live in groups of 3-10 individuals with a strict female dominance hierarchy, where the top female controls group movement.", "sifaka.jpg", "social"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "These sifakas eat over 100 different plant species throughout the year, switching between leaves, flowers, fruit, and bark depending on the season.", "sifaka.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Coquerel's Sifaka", "Fewer than 200,000 Coquerel's sifakas remain in the wild, restricted to fragmented forests in northwestern Madagascar.", "sifaka.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur ---
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "During hibernation, fat-tailed dwarf lemurs breathe only once every 20 minutes, and their heart rate drops from over 200 beats per minute to just 8.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "Fat-tailed dwarf lemurs hibernate in tree holes rather than underground burrows, making them unique among hibernating mammals.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "These tiny lemurs form monogamous pairs during the breeding season, with both parents participating in raising offspring.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Fat-tailed Dwarf Lemur", "Fat-tailed dwarf lemurs have a keen sense of smell and use scent marking to communicate, with males rubbing their heads on branches to leave chemical signals.", "dwarf_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Crowned Lemur ---
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs live in groups of 5-15 individuals led by dominant females who have priority access to food and choose where the group sleeps.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs can survive in highly degraded forest fragments and even plantations, making them more resilient than many other lemur species.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Female crowned lemurs typically give birth to a single infant, which rides on its mother's belly for the first three weeks before switching to her back.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Crowned lemurs have been observed eating soil from termite mounds, likely to supplement their diet with minerals and to aid in detoxifying plant compounds.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Crowned Lemur", "Their range is restricted to the northern tip of Madagascar, one of the smallest geographic ranges of any lemur species.", "crowned_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Golden Bamboo Lemur ---
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "Golden bamboo lemurs consume about 500 grams of bamboo per day, which contains roughly 12 times the lethal dose of cyanide for most mammals their size.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "These lemurs are most active at dawn and dusk, spending the middle of the day resting in dense bamboo thickets where predators cannot easily reach them.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "Golden bamboo lemurs live in small family groups of 2-6 individuals, with strong pair bonds between breeding adults.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "Climate change threatens golden bamboo lemurs by altering bamboo growth patterns, potentially reducing the availability of the young shoots they depend on.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Golden Bamboo Lemur", "The golden bamboo lemur has specialized teeth with thick enamel adapted for biting through tough bamboo culms that other lemurs cannot penetrate.", "bamboo_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Sportive Lemur ---
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Sportive lemurs are strictly nocturnal and can sometimes be spotted peering out of their sleeping holes during the day with their large, reflective eyes.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "A sportive lemur's territory can be as small as 0.3 hectares, one of the smallest home ranges of any primate.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Sportive lemurs communicate using specific calls at dusk before emerging from their tree holes, likely to coordinate territory boundaries with neighbors.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Female sportive lemurs park their single infant on a branch while foraging at night, returning periodically to nurse.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Sportive Lemur", "Several sportive lemur species have been discovered only through DNA analysis - they look nearly identical but are genetically as different as humans and chimpanzees.", "sportive_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Eastern Woolly Lemur ---
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Eastern woolly lemurs have an extremely low metabolic rate for their size, allowing them to survive on a nutrient-poor diet of leaves.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Woolly lemurs are strictly nocturnal, curling up in dense vine tangles or tree forks to sleep during the day, always in physical contact with their mate.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Pairs of eastern woolly lemurs defend territories of about 1-2 hectares using loud calls that carry through the forest at night.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "Female woolly lemurs give birth to a single infant once per year, and both parents carry and care for the young.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Eastern Woolly Lemur", "The eastern woolly lemur's large eyes have a reflective layer (tapetum lucidum) that gives them excellent night vision but makes them glow when caught in a flashlight beam.", "woolly_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Fork-marked Lemur ---
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Fork-marked lemurs travel up to 2 kilometers per night visiting specific gum-producing trees, following memorized routes through the canopy.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Their lower front teeth form a specialized 'tooth comb' that acts like a chisel, allowing them to gouge holes in tree bark to access gum.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Fork-marked lemurs are one of the fastest-moving nocturnal lemurs, capable of rapid quadrupedal running and leaping through the canopy.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "These lemurs produce a loud, repeated 'kee' call at dusk and dawn that can be heard up to 500 meters away, used to maintain spacing between individuals.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Fork-marked Lemur", "Fork-marked lemurs supplement their gum diet with nectar and insects, making them one of the few primate species to have such a specialized feeding niche.", "fork_marked_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Diademed Sifaka ---
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Diademed sifakas eat soil from cliff faces and termite mounds, a behavior called geophagy that helps neutralize toxins from the leaves they consume.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Groups of diademed sifakas maintain territories of 25-60 hectares, among the largest home ranges of any lemur species.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Female diademed sifakas give birth to a single infant every 2-3 years, with the baby clinging to its mother's fur from its first moments.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Diademed sifakas have thick, silky fur that repels rainwater - an essential adaptation for life in Madagascar's wet eastern rainforests.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Diademed Sifaka", "Their name comes from the white band of fur across the forehead, which resembles a diadem or crown.", "diademed_sifaka.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Black Lemur ---
            ("Black Lemur", "Black lemurs are cathemeral - active both during the day and at night - shifting their patterns based on moonlight, season, and food availability.", "black_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Black Lemur", "Black lemurs have a gestation period of about 120 days, after which females give birth to a single infant during the rainy season when food is abundant.", "black_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Black Lemur", "Groups of black lemurs are led by dominant females who decide when and where the group moves, feeds, and rests.", "black_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Black Lemur", "Black lemurs play an important ecological role as seed dispersers, with over 50 plant species depending on them to spread their seeds.", "black_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Black Lemur", "On Nosy Komba island, local Sakalava people consider black lemurs sacred and have taboos (fady) against harming them, providing informal conservation protection.", "black_lemur.jpg", "culture"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Mongoose Lemur ---
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Mongoose lemurs are one of the few lemur species that live in small family units of a mated pair and their offspring, rather than larger social groups.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Male and female mongoose lemurs have dramatically different facial hair - males have bushy red cheek whiskers while females have white cheek patches.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Mongoose lemurs on the Comoros Islands show behavioral differences from Madagascar populations, being more nocturnal and having smaller home ranges.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "Female mongoose lemurs give birth to a single infant after a gestation of about 128 days, timing births to the start of the wet season.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Mongoose Lemur", "The mongoose lemur's population on the Comoros Islands is critically threatened by deforestation for agriculture, with less than 5% of original forest remaining.", "mongoose_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- NEW FACTS: Pygmy Mouse Lemur ---
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Pygmy mouse lemurs build tiny spherical nests from leaves in dense vegetation, typically no larger than a grapefruit.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Female pygmy mouse lemurs can give birth to twins or triplets after a gestation of just 54 days, one of the shortest among primates.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Pygmy mouse lemurs communicate using ultrasonic calls above 20 kHz that are inaudible to most predators, giving them a secret communication channel.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Pygmy Mouse Lemur", "Despite weighing less than a AA battery, pygmy mouse lemurs can enter daily torpor to save energy, dropping their body temperature to match their surroundings.", "pygmy_mouse_lemur.jpg", "biology"),
            # General Madagascar facts
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "All lemurs are endemic to Madagascar - they exist naturally nowhere else on Earth. They evolved in isolation for over 60 million years after Madagascar split from Africa.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Indri", "Over 100 species of lemur have been identified, making them the most diverse group of primates relative to the small area of Madagascar.", "indri.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Lemurs arrived in Madagascar by rafting across the Mozambique Channel on floating vegetation approximately 60-70 million years ago.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            ("Aye-aye", "Over 90% of lemur species are threatened with extinction, making them the most endangered group of mammals on Earth.", "aye_aye.jpg", "conservation"),
            # General Madagascar/Lemur facts
            ("Ring-tailed Lemur", "Madagascar is the world's fourth-largest island and broke away from the Indian subcontinent about 88 million years ago, creating a unique evolutionary laboratory.", "ring_tailed.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Indri", "The word 'lemur' comes from the Latin 'lemures', meaning ghosts or spirits, because of their haunting nocturnal calls and reflective eyes.", "indri.jpg", "culture"),
            ("Mouse Lemur", "Madagascar has lost approximately 90% of its original forest cover, making habitat restoration critical for the survival of all lemur species.", "mouse_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Aye-aye", "Lemurs exhibit female social dominance in most species, a trait extremely rare among primates and found in less than 10% of mammal species overall.", "aye_aye.jpg", "social"),
            ("Verreaux's Sifaka", "The fossa, Madagascar's largest predator, is the primary natural threat to lemurs. It is a cat-like carnivore related to the mongoose.", "sifaka.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red Ruffed Lemur", "Community-based conservation programs in Madagascar pay local villagers to protect lemur habitat, turning former hunters into forest guardians.", "red_ruffed.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- Greater Bamboo Lemur ---
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "The greater bamboo lemur was thought to be extinct until a small population was rediscovered in 1972 in southeastern Madagascar.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "science"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Greater bamboo lemurs have the most powerful jaws relative to body size of any lemur, enabling them to crack open giant bamboo culms.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Fossils show that greater bamboo lemurs once ranged across all of Madagascar, but are now confined to a few tiny pockets of eastern rainforest.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Greater bamboo lemurs live in small groups of 4-7 individuals with a dominant breeding pair, and members share bamboo feeding sites.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "social"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Female greater bamboo lemurs give birth to a single infant after a gestation of about 149 days, one of the longest of any lemur.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Greater bamboo lemurs eat earth from stream banks and termite mounds, likely to supplement minerals and neutralize plant toxins.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Fewer than 500 greater bamboo lemurs remain in the wild, making them one of the 25 most endangered primates on Earth.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "The greater bamboo lemur's diet of giant bamboo makes it particularly vulnerable to climate change, which is altering bamboo flowering cycles.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Greater Bamboo Lemur", "Greater bamboo lemurs communicate with a repertoire of at least 7 distinct vocalizations, including loud calls to coordinate group movement through dense bamboo.", "greater_bamboo_lemur.jpg", "behavior"),
            # --- Red-bellied Lemur ---
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Red-bellied lemurs are one of the few truly monogamous lemur species, with mated pairs staying together for life.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Males have distinctive teardrop-shaped bare skin patches below their eyes that become more prominent during the breeding season.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Both parents carry and care for infants, with fathers doing as much infant-carrying as mothers - a rare trait among primates.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Red-bellied lemurs are cathemeral, switching between daytime and nighttime activity depending on the season and moonlight.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "They are important seed dispersers in montane rainforests, with seeds from over 40 plant species found in their droppings.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Red-bellied lemurs are one of the few lemur species found at elevations above 2000 meters, tolerating cool mountain conditions.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Family groups of red-bellied lemurs use soft contact calls to stay in touch while foraging in dense vegetation.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Red-bellied Lemur", "Red-bellied lemurs have a surprisingly varied diet that shifts with the seasons, eating over 70 different plant species throughout the year.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            # --- Collared Brown Lemur ---
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Males have a striking bushy orange-brown 'collar' of fur around the neck, while females have more subdued grey-brown fur.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Collared brown lemurs are one of the most important seed dispersers in southeastern Madagascar, passing viable seeds for over 50 plant species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "These lemurs live in groups of 3-12 individuals with flexible social hierarchies and relatively low levels of aggression.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Collared brown lemurs are found in littoral (coastal) forests, one of the most endangered habitats in all of Madagascar.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "They have been observed rubbing millipedes on their fur, possibly to use the millipedes' toxic secretions as an insect repellent.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Females give birth to a single infant during the austral spring, timed with the beginning of the fruit-rich wet season.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Collared brown lemurs have been introduced to two small islands near Manombo, creating insurance populations against extinction.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Collared Brown Lemur", "Their cathemeral activity pattern means researchers can observe them active at virtually any hour of the day or night.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            # --- White-fronted Brown Lemur ---
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "Males have a distinctive bright white forehead and face, while females are uniformly grey-brown, making this one of the most sexually dichromatic lemurs.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "First scientifically described in 1796, it is one of the earliest lemur species known to Western science.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "White-fronted brown lemurs live in multi-male, multi-female groups of 3-12 individuals with relatively egalitarian social structures.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "They play an important role as seed dispersers in northeastern Madagascar's rainforests, consuming fruit from over 60 tree species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "White-fronted brown lemurs have been observed eating soil and bark, likely to supplement their mineral intake.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "Females typically give birth to a single infant, which clings to the mother's belly for the first few weeks before riding on her back.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "Their range overlaps with several other Eulemur species, and occasional natural hybridization has been documented in contact zones.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("White-fronted Brown Lemur", "White-fronted brown lemurs are cathemeral and adjust their activity patterns based on season, temperature, and fruit availability.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            # --- Milne-Edwards' Sifaka ---
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Named after French zoologist Alphonse Milne-Edwards, this sifaka has been studied continuously at Ranomafana since the 1980s.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Milne-Edwards' sifakas eat soil from termite mounds, a behavior that helps detoxify the defensive chemicals in the leaves they consume.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "They live in groups of 3-9 individuals, and females are dominant over males, choosing preferred feeding sites and sleeping trees.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Females give birth to a single infant every two years, and infants ride on their mother's back starting at about 3 weeks of age.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Their dark brown to black fur distinguishes them from most other sifakas, which tend toward white or golden coloring.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Milne-Edwards' sifakas maintain large home ranges of 30-60 hectares and travel an average of 1 kilometer per day.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "Long-term research has shown that infanticide by immigrant males is a significant source of infant mortality in this species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "They can leap over 8 meters between trees, rotating mid-air to land upright on vertical trunks in the dense rainforest.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Milne-Edwards' Sifaka", "The creation of Ranomafana National Park in 1991 was partly motivated by the need to protect this sifaka's remaining habitat.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- Silky Sifaka ---
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Known as the 'angel of the forest,' the silky sifaka's luminous white fur appears to glow against the dark green of the rainforest canopy.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Fewer than 250 silky sifakas remain in the wild, restricted to a few high-altitude rainforest fragments in northeastern Madagascar.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Like the indri, silky sifakas have never been successfully kept in captivity, making protection of wild habitat their only hope for survival.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Silky sifakas eat over 100 different plant species throughout the year, switching between leaves, seeds, fruit, and flowers with the seasons.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "They live in small groups of 2-9 individuals and communicate with soft contact calls while moving through the canopy.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Females give birth to a single infant only every 2-3 years, one of the slowest reproductive rates of any lemur.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Local taboos (fady) protect silky sifakas in some communities, where killing them is believed to bring misfortune.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "culture"),
            ("Silky Sifaka", "Silky sifakas are found only above 700 meters elevation, making them vulnerable to climate change pushing habitable zones higher.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            # --- Greater Dwarf Lemur ---
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Greater dwarf lemurs hibernate for 3-5 months during the cool dry season, relying on fat stored throughout their body.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Unlike the fat-tailed dwarf lemur, which stores fat primarily in its tail, the greater dwarf lemur distributes fat reserves across its body and limbs.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "During hibernation, their body temperature can drop to within 1 degree of the ambient temperature, similar to reptilian thermoregulation.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Greater dwarf lemurs are nocturnal and solitary foragers, but may sleep in groups of up to 5 individuals during the day.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "They have large, forward-facing eyes that give them excellent stereoscopic night vision for navigating the forest canopy.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Females typically give birth to twins or triplets after a gestation of about 70 days, one of the highest litter sizes among lemurs.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Greater dwarf lemurs play an important role as pollinators, visiting flowers for nectar and transferring pollen on their fur.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Greater Dwarf Lemur", "Research on greater dwarf lemur hibernation has provided insights into how primates can survive extended periods of metabolic suppression.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            # --- Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur ---
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "The hairy-eared dwarf lemur was thought to be extinct until its rediscovery in 1989, after not being seen since the 1870s.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "It is the sole member of the genus Allocebus, making it evolutionarily unique with no close living relatives.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "The distinctive long tufts of hair protruding from its ears give this species its common name and help distinguish it from other dwarf lemurs.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "Hairy-eared dwarf lemurs specialize in feeding on tree gum, using their tooth combs to gouge bark and access the sticky sap.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "They weigh only 75-98 grams, making them one of the smallest primates and small enough to fit in a cupped hand.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "So little is known about their reproduction in the wild that basic information like gestation length remains uncertain.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "Hairy-eared dwarf lemurs are strictly nocturnal, emerging from their tree-hole sleeping sites only after complete darkness.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Hairy-eared Dwarf Lemur", "Their extreme rarity means that every confirmed sighting contributes valuable data to scientific understanding of this enigmatic species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            # --- Northern Sportive Lemur ---
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "With fewer than 50 individuals remaining, the northern sportive lemur is one of the rarest primates on Earth.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Its entire remaining habitat consists of a few tiny forest fragments at the extreme northern tip of Madagascar.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Northern sportive lemurs are strictly nocturnal and spend the day hidden in tree holes, emerging only after dark to feed on leaves.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Bushmeat hunting poses a severe threat to this species, as its predictable sleeping sites make it easy for hunters to locate.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Like other sportive lemurs, they practice cecotrophy - re-ingesting feces to extract maximum nutrients from their leaf diet.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Females typically give birth to a single infant during the wet season, parking it on a branch while foraging.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "Conservation groups are working to connect the remaining forest fragments with corridors to allow gene flow between isolated populations.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Northern Sportive Lemur", "The northern sportive lemur was only recognized as a distinct species in 2006, based on genetic and morphological analysis.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            # --- Weasel Sportive Lemur ---
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "The weasel sportive lemur was the first sportive lemur described by science, in 1851, and serves as the type species for the genus.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "taxonomy"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Its name comes from its weasel-like appearance, with a long body, short limbs, and a somewhat pointed face.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Weasel sportive lemurs are solitary and territorial, with males and females maintaining overlapping home ranges.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "They inhabit eastern rainforests from sea level to 1800 meters, giving them one of the broadest elevational ranges of any sportive lemur.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Their large, round eyes with a reflective tapetum lucidum give them exceptional night vision for navigating the dark understory.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Weasel sportive lemurs communicate with soft vocalizations at dusk, likely to establish spacing between neighboring individuals.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Like all sportive lemurs, they have a specialized digestive system for breaking down the cellulose in their leaf-heavy diet.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Weasel Sportive Lemur", "Despite being one of the longest-known sportive lemurs, basic aspects of its ecology and behavior remain poorly studied.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            # --- Grey Bamboo Lemur ---
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "The grey bamboo lemur is the most common and widespread of all bamboo lemur species, found across much of eastern Madagascar.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Unlike the golden bamboo lemur, the grey bamboo lemur eats new bamboo shoots and leaves that contain far lower levels of cyanide.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Grey bamboo lemurs live in small family groups of 3-5 individuals and are active primarily during the day.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "They have specialized teeth adapted for stripping and chewing bamboo, with enlarged premolars for gripping tough plant material.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Grey bamboo lemurs communicate with soft clicks and purring sounds that help maintain group cohesion in dense bamboo stands.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Females give birth to a single infant after a gestation of about 137 days, typically during the wet season when bamboo growth peaks.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Their dependence on bamboo makes them vulnerable to habitat loss, as bamboo stands are often the first vegetation cleared for agriculture.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Grey Bamboo Lemur", "Grey bamboo lemurs occasionally supplement their diet with fruit, flowers, and fungi when bamboo shoots are not available.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            # --- Red-tailed Sportive Lemur ---
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Named for its distinctive reddish-brown tail that contrasts with its grey-brown body fur, making it identifiable among sportive lemurs.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Red-tailed sportive lemurs share their dry forest habitat with Verreaux's sifaka, occupying different ecological niches as nocturnal folivores.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "They sleep in tree holes during the day, often returning to the same hole for weeks or months at a time.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Red-tailed sportive lemurs are strictly solitary outside of the breeding season, with each individual defending a small territory.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Their home ranges are among the smallest of any primate, often less than half a hectare.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Females give birth to a single infant, which is parked on branches while the mother forages nearby.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "The Kirindy Forest, where red-tailed sportive lemurs are best studied, is also home to the fossa, their primary predator.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-tailed Sportive Lemur", "Like other sportive lemurs, they have an enlarged cecum that functions as a fermentation chamber for breaking down tough leaves.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            # --- FACTS FOR 11 NEW SPECIES ---
            # Red-fronted Lemur
            ("Red-fronted Lemur", "Red-fronted lemurs are one of the few lemur species that can thrive in degraded and fragmented forests, making them more resilient than many other species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-fronted Lemur", "Males and females look nearly identical, with both having the characteristic reddish-brown forehead patch that gives the species its name.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Red-fronted Lemur", "These lemurs form groups of 4-18 individuals with a flexible social structure where both males and females may disperse from their natal groups.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Red-fronted Lemur", "Red-fronted lemurs play a crucial role in seed dispersal, with studies showing they can disperse seeds up to 500 meters from parent trees.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Red-fronted Lemur", "Females give birth to a single infant after a gestation of about 120 days, timing births to coincide with the start of the rainy season when food is abundant.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # Common Brown Lemur
            ("Common Brown Lemur", "Common brown lemurs show remarkable geographic variation in coat color, with some populations appearing grey while others are more reddish or brown.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Common Brown Lemur", "This species is known for its adaptability and can be found in a wide range of habitats from dry deciduous forests to humid rainforests.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Common Brown Lemur", "Common brown lemurs are cathemeral, meaning they are active both during the day and at night, adjusting their activity patterns based on environmental conditions.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Common Brown Lemur", "They live in groups of 3-12 individuals with a relatively egalitarian social structure compared to other lemur species.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Common Brown Lemur", "Females typically give birth to a single infant, which clings to its mother's belly for the first few weeks before riding on her back.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # Sanford's Brown Lemur
            ("Sanford's Brown Lemur", "Sanford's brown lemurs are often seen in mixed-species groups with crowned lemurs, a rare example of regular interspecies association among primates.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Sanford's Brown Lemur", "Males have distinctive white ear tufts and a dark face mask, while females have more subdued facial markings.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Sanford's Brown Lemur", "This species is named after Leonard Cutler Sanford, a trustee of the American Museum of Natural History who funded early expeditions to Madagascar.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("Sanford's Brown Lemur", "Sanford's brown lemurs have a varied diet that includes fruit, flowers, leaves, and nectar, with seasonal shifts in food preferences.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            ("Sanford's Brown Lemur", "They are primarily arboreal but will occasionally descend to the ground to cross gaps between forest fragments.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            # Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur
            ("Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur", "The Lac Alaotra gentle lemur is the only primate in the world that lives exclusively in wetlands, feeding primarily on papyrus and reeds.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
            ("Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur", "With fewer than 2,500 individuals remaining, this is one of the 25 most endangered primates on Earth.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur", "Unlike other gentle lemurs, this species has adapted to life in marshes and can swim short distances to escape predators.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur", "Their habitat around Lake Alaotra is threatened by agricultural expansion, particularly rice farming, which is destroying the marshes they depend on.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "conservation"),
            ("Lac Alaotra Gentle Lemur", "Females give birth to a single infant, which clings to its mother as she moves through the dense reed beds.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "reproduction"),
            # Northern Giant Mouse Lemur
            ("Northern Giant Mouse Lemur", "Northern giant mouse lemurs are one of the largest mouse lemur species, weighing up to 300 grams - about the size of a small squirrel.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Northern Giant Mouse Lemur", "They have distinctive white ear tufts and loud vocalizations that carry through the forest at night.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Northern Giant Mouse Lemur", "Unlike smaller mouse lemurs, northern giant mouse lemurs often nest in tree holes rather than building leaf nests.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Northern Giant Mouse Lemur", "They form monogamous pairs during the breeding season, with both parents participating in raising offspring.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "social"),
            ("Northern Giant Mouse Lemur", "Northern giant mouse lemurs have a varied diet that includes fruit, flowers, nectar, insects, and even small vertebrates.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "biology"),
            # Coquerel's Giant Mouse Lemur
            ("Coquerel's Giant Mouse Lemur", "Coquerel's giant mouse lemurs are the largest of all mouse lemurs, with a bushy tail and distinctive facial markings.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "anatomy"),
            ("Coquerel's Giant Mouse Lemur", "They are named after French naturalist Charles Coquerel, who collected the first specimens in the 19th century.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "science"),
            ("Coquerel's Giant Mouse Lemur", "Unlike smaller mouse lemurs, Coquerel's giant mouse lemurs often nest in tree holes and have more complex social behaviors.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "behavior"),
            ("Coquerel's Giant Mouse Lemur", "They have a varied diet that includes fruit, gum, insects, and small vertebrates, making them important seed dispersers.", "madagascar_forest.jpg", "ecology"),
